    "go-structs-learn": {
      "id": "go-structs-learn",
      "title": "Learn: name the fields the fact is made of",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-structs"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Write the Order struct before the loop: a Cents on its own cannot say which order it belongs to. If the terminal says go is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "Total(nil) is not an error case, range over nil runs zero times, so the accumulator you started with is the answer."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Panicking on a nil slice, nil means no elements, and a total of no elements is 0.",
        "Passing the ID and the cents as two arguments that can get swapped, the struct is what keeps them one fact."
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain why an order is one struct with named fields rather than an ID and a number passed side by side.",
        "Trace Total over a nil slice and say why the sum stays at zero instead of panicking.",
        "Apply the rule that a nil slice and an empty slice are the same situation for a total."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "An order is not two unrelated variables that happen to sit next to each other. It is one value with fields: an ID and an amount in cents. A struct says that, and a function that takes []Order can only see what the struct allows. Loose strings and ints passed side by side drift apart the first time someone reorders the arguments."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The fact, and a total over a list of them:",
          "code": "type Order struct {\n    ID    string\n    Cents int\n}\n\nfunc Total(orders []Order) int {\n    sum := 0\n    for _, o := range orders {\n        sum += o.Cents\n    }\n    return sum\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A nil slice and an empty slice both have no elements. range over either visits nothing, and the sum stays 0. Panicking on nil is treating \"no orders\" as a crash, wrong for a total. The ID is part of the fact even when this function does not read it, because the next function will, and a struct built for one function gets rebuilt whenever the fact grows."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "Total is handed a []Order variable that was never assigned. What should it return?",
          "items": [
            "0, range over nil visits nothing, so nothing is added.",
            "It should panic, because a nil slice means the caller made a mistake.",
            "The length of the slice, which is 0 either way.",
            "-1, so the caller can tell nil apart from an empty slice."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "A nil slice has length 0 and no elements: range walks nothing, the accumulator keeps its zero value, and the total is 0, the same total an empty slice produces. Somebody with no orders is one situation, not two, and a sentinel like -1 would collide with a real total."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A struct names the fields of one fact. Functions take it, not arguments.",
            "range over a nil slice does nothing: no orders total 0.",
            "Even fields you do not read yet belong on the struct."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "order.go is where this phase works.",
          "code": "// Total sums Cents. A nil or empty slice totals 0.\nfunc Total(orders []Order) int {\n\treturn -1\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "go test ./...   # the command you run: every test in the module\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/order.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls for this phase"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Write the Order struct in order.go with ID and Cents before you write Total. Then say what Total of a nil slice is, and implement the loop so that answer holds."
        }
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Go Spec: struct types and field selectors",
          "url": "https://go.dev/ref/spec#Struct_types"
        }
      ],
      "bridge": {
        "course": "task-manager",
        "phase": "fundamentals",
        "text": "In JavaScript an object holds named fields and you find one by its key. A Go struct holds the same fields with their types written down, so a function that takes the struct can only see what the struct allows."
      }
    },
