    "go-methods-learn": {
      "id": "go-methods-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the receiver is how a type changes",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-methods"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Distinguish a value receiver from a pointer receiver by what each one is allowed to change.",
        "Trace a Deposit written on a value receiver and explain why the caller’s balance never moves.",
        "Apply the method name as a contract: refuse a deposit of zero or less and leave the balance alone."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A function that takes an Account and returns a new one copies the value. The caller's account is untouched unless they remember to use the return value. A method with a pointer receiver changes the account the caller is holding. Deposit has to be that kind of method, because a deposit that does not stick is not a deposit."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "Read with a value, write with a pointer:",
          "code": "func (a Account) Balance() int { return a.Cents }\n\nfunc (a *Account) Deposit(n int) {\n    if n > 0 {\n        a.Cents += n\n    }\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Balance does not need a pointer: it only reads. Deposit does, and it refuses a non-positive amount. Adding a negative \"deposit\" would be a withdrawal wearing the wrong name. Zero changes nothing, which is the edge, a call that looked like it happened and left the balance where it was."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "Balance only reads the account. Deposit changes it. Which receiver does Deposit need?",
          "items": [
            "A value receiver, because a small struct is cheaper to copy.",
            "A pointer receiver, because the change must reach the caller.",
            "A value receiver, because Deposit hands nothing back to the caller.",
            "Either one. Go promotes the receiver to a pointer when a field is assigned."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explain": "A value receiver is a copy. Deposit would add to that copy and the caller would still hold the old cents, which is why the star is not decoration. Balance may read through a copy because it never writes."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A value receiver sees a copy. A pointer receiver sees the caller's value.",
            "Mutation belongs on the pointer; forgetting the star stalls the balance.",
            "A zero-or-less deposit is not a deposit; leave the balance alone."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "account.go is where this phase works.",
          "code": "// Balance reports the cents on the account.\nfunc (a Account) Balance() int {\n\treturn -1\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "go test ./...   # the command you run: every test in the module\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/account.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls for this phase"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "On Account, write Balance so it returns Cents, and Deposit so a second call sees the first call's money. Then call Deposit(0) on paper and confirm the balance did not move."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Deposit must change the Account the caller holds; check whether the receiver is that value or a copy of it. If the terminal says go is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "Balance can read through a copy, but Deposit cannot write through one, the star in the receiver is the difference."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Using a value receiver for a method that must update the caller, the update lands on a copy and the caller keeps the old cents.",
        "Treating a negative deposit as a withdrawal, the method's name is the contract, and this one only adds."
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "value receiver",
            "lines": [
              "Balance() reads a copy",
              "the copy is enough",
              "the caller keeps 500"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "pointer receiver",
            "lines": [
              "Deposit(100) writes through",
              "the same Account",
              "now holds 600"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "A value receiver works on a copy, so the caller keeps 500. A pointer receiver writes through, and the same Account holds 600."
      },
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Effective Go: pointees vs. value receivers",
          "url": "https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#pointers_vs_values"
        }
      ],
      "bridge": {
        "course": "task-manager",
        "phase": "fundamentals",
        "text": "In JavaScript a function is a value you pass around, and it belongs to nothing. A Go method belongs to a type and receives the value it works on, and whether it receives a copy or the address decides what it can change."
      }
    },
