    "go-packages-learn": {
      "id": "go-packages-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the import is the boundary",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-packages"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what makes a name visible across a package boundary, and why the compiler is the gate.",
        "Trace how Checkout reaches money.Cents through an import instead of formatting the value itself.",
        "Apply the boundary: put one formatting rule in its own package and call it from the other."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Everything so far lived in package ledger, one boundary. Money formatting is a different concern, and it gets its own directory and package name. Code outside that directory can call only names that start with a capital letter. The compiler enforces the door, not a comment that says \"please do not use this\"."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The other package, and the only way in:",
          "code": "package money\n\nfunc Cents(n int) string { /* ... */ }\n\n// in package ledger\nimport \"example.com/ledger/money\"\n\nfunc Checkout(cents int) string {\n    return money.Cents(cents)\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Checkout does not format anything itself. If it does, the two copies drift: one gains a sign, the other forgets it, and the receipt disagrees with the package that was supposed to own the rule. The test calls money.Cents directly and calls Checkout. Both have to agree, which only happens when Checkout goes through the import."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A package is a directory with one name, imported by path plus folder.",
            "Exported names start with a capital; the rest is invisible outside.",
            "One rule lives in one package; callers import it, not reimplement it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "money/money.go is where this phase works.",
          "code": "// Cents renders whole cents. 199 is \"1.99\". -5 is \"-0.05\".\nfunc Cents(n int) string {\n\treturn \"\"\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "go test ./...   # the command you run: every test in the module\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/money.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls for this phase"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open money/money.go and implement Cents there. Then make Checkout in checkout.go return money.Cents(cents). Run the tests from the module root so both packages are built together."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Checkout lives in another package: only a capital letter lets a name cross that boundary, whatever the file is called. If the terminal says go is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "If the compiler says undefined, read the name you wrote, the import path is the directory, the export is the first letter."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Reimplementing the formatter inside Checkout, the package boundary is only real if the caller crosses it.",
        "Assuming the same module means every name is visible, lowercase names stop at the package, and the compiler is the thing that stops them."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Effective Go: packages",
          "url": "https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#packages"
        }
      ]
    },
    "go-packages-ask": {
      "id": "go-packages-ask",
      "title": "Reason: why the helper is invisible",
      "kind": "question",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-packages"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "money.go has func cents(n int) string, with a lowercase c. Checkout tries to call money.cents. What happens?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "It works if Checkout is in a file that starts with package money.",
        "It does not compile, a lowercase name is not exported to another package.",
        "It compiles and panics at run time, because the function exists but is private.",
        "It works, the packages are in the same module, so every name is shared."
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Export is a compile-time rule based on the first letter: a capital letter is visible to importers, a lowercase one only inside its own package, and the module has no say in it. The failure is a compiler error, not a panic, because the boundary is checked before the program runs. If Checkout is declared package money, it is the same package, not an import.",
      "hints": [
        "The first letter of the function name decides visibility; the directory and the module have no say in it."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Reimplementing the formatter inside Checkout, the package boundary is only real if the caller crosses it.",
        "Assuming the same module means every name is visible, lowercase names stop at the package, and the compiler is the thing that stops them."
      ]
    },
