    "go-embedding-learn": {
      "id": "go-embedding-learn",
      "title": "Learn: embedding is composition that promotes",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-embedding"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what embedding promotes from an inner type into an outer one, and why it is composition rather than inheritance.",
        "Trace a call to a promoted method and say which value answers it.",
        "Apply the shadowing rule to predict which method runs when the outer and inner types both define it."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Go has no classes to inherit from, so it composes. You put one type inside another without naming the field, and that is embedding. The inner type's exported fields and methods are lifted up, promoted, so the outer value answers them as if they were its own."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Why it matters: Embedding is Go's primary way to build larger systems from smaller components. A service that embeds its dependencies can swap them out without touching the service code. Embedding without understanding promotion leads to hidden methods, shadowed implementations, and tests that fail when the embedded type changes. This phase teaches you the rules so embedding becomes a deliberate design choice, not a surprise."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "An embedded type, and a method it inherits for free:",
          "code": "type Kernel struct{ N int }\n\nfunc (k Kernel) Twice() int { return k.N * 2 }\n\ntype Satellite struct {\n    Kernel   // embedded: no field name\n    Bonus int\n}\n\ns := Satellite{Kernel{N: 5}, Bonus: 3}\ns.Twice()   // promoted from Kernel, so 10\ns.N         // the embedded field is reachable by name too"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "s.N works because an embedded field takes the embedded type's name as its own, and its fields show through. If Satellite defined its own Twice, that one would win, the outer method shadows the promoted one, and you can still reach the inner as s.Kernel.Twice(). That is composition, not a parent class."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "Embedding is a field with no name; its methods promote to outer type.",
            "The outer type answers promoted methods as its own, satisfying interfaces for free.",
            "An outer method shadows a promoted one; reach the inner by type name."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "embed.go is where this phase works.",
          "code": "// Sum returns the promoted Twice plus the bonus.\nfunc (s Satellite) Sum() int {\n\treturn -1\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "go test ./...   # the command you run: every test in the module\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/embed.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls for this phase"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "In embed.go, Satellite embeds Kernel. Write Sum so it calls the promoted Twice and adds Bonus, do not recompute the doubling yourself."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Write both numbers down before you finish the method: Sum of Kernel{5} with a Bonus of 3 is 13, not 8. If the terminal says go is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "A promoted method is already on the value. Call s.Twice(); do not write s.N * 2 again."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Reimplementing the promoted method instead of calling it, the whole point of embedding is that Twice is already there.",
        "Giving the embedded type a field name (k Kernel) and then wondering why s.Twice() no longer resolves, a named field is not an embedded one."
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "component-tree",
        "spec": {
          "root": "s: Satellite",
          "props": [
            "s.Twice() gives 10"
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "root": "Kernel",
              "props": [
                "embedded - no field name",
                "Twice() promoted from it"
              ],
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "root": "Bonus int",
              "props": [
                "named field - s.Bonus"
              ],
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "The Kernel is embedded, so its methods promote to s. Bonus is a named field, so it is reached as s.Bonus and nothing is promoted."
      },
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Effective Go: embedding",
          "url": "https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#embedding"
        }
      ]
    },
    "go-embedding-ask": {
      "id": "go-embedding-ask",
      "title": "Reason: which Twice runs",
      "kind": "question",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-embedding"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Satellite embeds Kernel, which has Twice. Someone adds func (s Satellite) Twice() int { return s.N * 3 }. A Satellite holding N=4 calls Twice(). What comes back?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "A compile error, a type cannot declare a method with the same name as a promoted one.",
        "16. Go adds the outer and inner results together.",
        "8, the embedded Kernel method is the one attached to the inner value.",
        "12, the outer type's own Twice shadows the promoted one."
      ],
      "answer": 3,
      "explanation": "When the outer type declares a method with the same name as a promoted one, the outer method wins: embedding places the inner method set one level further out than its own names, so the outer shadows rather than clashes. It is not a redefinition error. The promoted Twice still exists as s.Kernel.Twice(), but a plain s.Twice() resolves to the outer one, 4 * 3 = 12.",
      "hints": [
        "A method the type declares itself sits closer than one it only inherited."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Reimplementing the promoted method instead of calling it, the whole point of embedding is that Twice is already there.",
        "Giving the embedded type a field name (k Kernel) and then wondering why s.Twice() no longer resolves, a named field is not an embedded one."
      ]
    },
