    "go-generics-learn": {
      "id": "go-generics-learn",
      "title": "Learn: one function, many types",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-generics"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what a type parameter stands for and what its constraint allows the body to do.",
        "Trace one generic function called with ints and with strings, and say why one body serves both.",
        "Apply cmp.Ordered to a function that compares values, and explain why any would refuse the comparison.",
        "Predict what a generic Max returns for an empty slice, and why that is the zero value."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Before generics you wrote a helper for ints, then copied it for float64, then for strings, several bodies doing the same loop. A type parameter turns that into one function that stands for many: you declare a placeholder type, name the set of types it may hold (its constraint), then write the body once against the placeholder."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "One function that works for any ordered type, using the comparison the constraint grants:",
          "code": "func Largest[T cmp.Ordered](a, b T) T {\n    if a > b {\n        return a\n    }\n    return b\n}\n\nLargest(3, 9)       // T inferred as int, so 9\nLargest(\"a\", \"b\")   // the same body over strings, so \"b\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The brackets after the name hold the type parameter: T is not a value, it is a type the caller chooses. cmp.Ordered is the constraint; it lists the types allowed (ints, floats, strings) and, crucially, hands the body the comparison operators. Call Largest(3, 9) and Go infers T=int; call it with strings and the identical compiled logic runs over them."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A type parameter is a placeholder; the constraint bounds what fills it.",
            "The constraint grants the body its operations, not just a type whitelist.",
            "Go infers the argument from the call; write it only when inference fails."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "gen.go is where this phase works.",
          "code": "// Max returns the largest value in xs, or the zero value of T when xs is empty.\nfunc Max[T cmp.Ordered](xs []T) T {\n\tpanic(\"unimplemented\")\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "go test ./...   # the command you run: every test in the module\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/gen.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls for this phase"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "In gen.go, implement Max so it returns the largest value in xs using the comparison cmp.Ordered already grants, and returns the zero value of T for an empty slice."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read the signature first: Max[T cmp.Ordered](xs []T) T means the caller picks a type T, and the body works on values of that T. If the terminal says go is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "cmp.Ordered already grants the < and > operators, so you can compare two values of T directly without any assertion."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Trying to add or compare a type parameter before its constraint says that is allowed, the constraint is what unlocks the operators for T.",
        "Writing two near-identical functions, one for int and one for string, instead of a single generic that covers both."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Generics tutorial",
          "url": "https://go.dev/doc/tutorial/generics"
        }
      ]
    },
    "go-generics-ask": {
      "id": "go-generics-ask",
      "title": "Reason: what the constraint buys",
      "kind": "question",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-generics"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "You write func pick[T any](xs []T) T and the body tries if xs[0] < xs[1]. The compiler refuses. Why, and what is the smallest fix?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "Because < is never allowed inside a generic function, whether the type is ordered or not.",
        "Because T might have no order; change the constraint to cmp.Ordered, the set that supports < and >.",
        "Because you must write xs[0].(int) before comparing any two elements of the slice.",
        "Because generic code cannot index a slice, so you have to use a range loop with an accumulator variable."
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "A type parameter is only as capable as its constraint. any (the empty interface) promises nothing, so the compiler cannot assume < exists for T. cmp.Ordered lists the ordered types and grants the comparison operators to the body. Swapping any for cmp.Ordered is the minimal fix, not a type assertion, and indexing a slice is fine in generic code.",
      "hints": [
        "Ask what the constraint promised. any promises nothing; the < operator needs an ordered constraint."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Trying to add or compare a type parameter before its constraint says that is allowed, the constraint is what unlocks the operators for T.",
        "Writing two near-identical functions, one for int and one for string, instead of a single generic that covers both."
      ]
    },
